Relax numerical tolerances in torchaudio tests on some platforms.

With PyTorch 2.9.1, a small number of torchaudio 2.9.1 tests show
platform-dependent floating-point differences on some systems. The same tests
pass on other Intel and AMD systems.

Relax tolerances only for the affected librosa compatibility tests and the
Spectrogram, InverseSpectrogram, and PitchShift batch-consistency checks. Keep
the default strict tolerances for all other batch-consistency tests.

On JSC, the batch pitch-shift difference is at most about 3.5e-6, while the
spectrogram test differs in only one out of 49446 values by about 5.3e-8.

Author: Pavel Tomanek (Inuits/UGent) with help from ChatGPT5.6
--- a/test/torchaudio_unittest/functional/librosa_compatibility_test_impl.py
+++ b/test/torchaudio_unittest/functional/librosa_compatibility_test_impl.py
@@ -98 +98 @@
-        self.assertEqual(result, torch.from_numpy(expected))
+        self.assertEqual(result, torch.from_numpy(expected), atol=1e-5, rtol=1e-5)
@@ -108 +108 @@
-        self.assertEqual(result, torch.from_numpy(expected))
+        self.assertEqual(result, torch.from_numpy(expected), atol=1e-5, rtol=1e-5)
--- a/test/torchaudio_unittest/transforms/batch_consistency_test.py
+++ b/test/torchaudio_unittest/transforms/batch_consistency_test.py
@@ -24,4 +24,9 @@
         batch_result = transform(batch_input, *args, **kwargs)

+        if isinstance(transform, T.Spectrogram):
+            atol = max(atol, 1e-7)
+        elif isinstance(transform, (T.InverseSpectrogram, T.PitchShift)):
+            atol = max(atol, 5e-6)
+
         self.assertEqual(items_input, batch_input, rtol=rtol, atol=atol)
         self.assertEqual(items_result, batch_result, rtol=rtol, atol=atol)
